  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "channel-creation",
      "text": "NewTimer(d) allocates a channel with capacity 1, passes that same channel to the runtime as the send argument and through syncTimer, then exposes it as t.C. After(d) constructs a timer through NewTimer and returns its C. The documentation describes the Go 1.23 channel as synchronous with capacity 0 and says an unreferenced, unstopped timer can be collected before expiration.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 111,
        "fragment": "c := make(chan Time, 1)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: What runtime behavior reconciles the capacity-1 allocation with the documented observable capacity of 0, and under which runtime settings does that guarantee apply?"
    },
    {
      "id": "channel-stop-order",
      "text": "For a running NewTimer timer under the documented Go 1.23 behavior, if Stop returns before any receive has occurred, Stop returns true and a subsequent receive blocks while the timer remains stopped. If a receive wins first and consumes the expiration, Stop returns false. A second Stop after a successful Stop also returns false if no Reset intervenes. Stop on a zero-value Timer panics before calling the runtime.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 80,
        "fragment": "if the program has not received from t.C already and the timer is"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "channel-reset-order",
      "text": "For a NewTimer timer under the documented Go 1.23 behavior, Reset on a still-running timer before any receive returns true and reschedules the same timer on the same C. If the old expiration has already been received, or the timer has been stopped, Reset returns false and still schedules a new expiration. A receive after Reset returns cannot obtain a value from the previous settings. Reset on a zero-value Timer panics before computing a deadline.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 143,
        "fragment": "return resetTimer(t, w, 0)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "callback-stop-order",
      "text": "AfterFunc creates a timer with nil C whose expiration launches f in a separate goroutine. If Stop wins while the timer is still active, it returns true and prevents that launch. If expiration wins, Stop returns false and may return while f is still running. If Stop already succeeded and no Reset intervenes, a second Stop also returns false even though f was never launched.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 71,
        "fragment": "if t.Stop returns false, then the timer has already expired"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the callback-specific explanation of a false Stop result be limited to cases where expiration won, given that a prior successful Stop also makes a later Stop return false?"
    },
    {
      "id": "callback-reset-order",
      "text": "For an AfterFunc timer, Reset before expiration while it is active returns true and moves the pending invocation to the new deadline. After a successful Stop, Reset returns false and schedules f on the same timer even if f has never run. After expiration, Reset also returns false and schedules another invocation. Reset returns without waiting for the prior invocation, so the two invocations can overlap if the prior one is still running when the next begins.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 124,
        "fragment": "When Reset returns false, Reset neither waits for the prior f to"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should “run again” be limited to the expiration case, since Reset after a successful Stop can schedule the first invocation?"
    },
    {
      "id": "deferred-channel-send",
      "text": "If the runtime defers sendTime past the intended send time and passes the elapsed delay as delta, sendTime computes Now minus delta when invoked. A successful send can therefore deliver a timestamp earlier than the actual send or receive. If the send cannot proceed immediately, the default branch returns without delivering that value.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 154,
        "fragment": "case c.(chan Time) <- Now().Add(Duration(-delta)):"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Does “current time” in the NewTimer and After documentation mean the scheduled firing time when delivery is deferred?"
    }
  ]
}
